      content: 'A 42-year-old right-handed female elementary school teacher presents to the PM&R clinic with severe right hand and wrist pain, swelling, and color changes 3 months after an uncomplicated closed reduction and casting of a right distal radius fracture sustained from a fall on an outstretched hand. She reports that the pain never improved after cast removal — instead, it dramatically worsened. She describes constant burning, throbbing pain rated 8-9/10 that is disproportionate to any remaining injury. She cannot tolerate clothing sleeves touching her forearm, water from the shower feels like fire on her hand, and she is unable to use her dominant hand for writing, typing, or self-care. She has not been able to return to teaching. Her employer\'s HR department sent her a letter requesting she return to work or provide documentation of disability, and her workers\' compensation adjuster has questioned the legitimacy of her claim, stating: "The fracture healed normally on X-ray — there is no reason she can\'t work." She is tearful and frustrated: "Everyone thinks I\'m faking this. My own family doesn\'t believe me. The pain is real."',
