    const Section(
      title: 'Physical Examination Findings:',
      content: '**Vitals**: BP 138/84 mmHg (may reflect pain), HR 82 bpm, RR 16, Temp 36.9C.\n\n**General**: Alert, guarding the right hand and wrist. Holds the right upper extremity protectively against her body. Appears distressed. Tearful during examination.\n\n**Right Upper Extremity Examination**:\n- **Inspection**: Diffuse edema of the right hand and wrist extending to mid-forearm. Skin is erythematous with mottled discoloration. Shiny, taut appearance of the dorsal hand skin. Nail changes — right fingernails are ridged and grow faster than the left. Increased hair growth on the right dorsal hand compared to the left.\n- **Temperature**: Right hand palpably warmer than the left. Infrared thermometry: right dorsal hand 34.2C, left dorsal hand 31.8C (>1C asymmetry is significant; this is >2C asymmetry).\n- **Edema**: Non-pitting edema of right hand and fingers. Figure-of-eight measurement: right hand 2.3 cm greater than left.\n- **Allodynia testing**: Light touch with cotton wisp to the right dorsal hand elicits severe pain (rated 7/10). Light brush stroke across the right forearm causes withdrawal and grimacing. Allodynia extends beyond the original fracture site to involve the entire hand, wrist, and distal forearm.\n- **Hyperalgesia**: Pinprick with Wartenberg wheel — dramatically amplified pain response on right hand compared to left.\n- **Motor**: Right grip strength 8 kg (left 28 kg). Right pinch strength <1 kg (left 6 kg). Active ROM of right wrist: flexion 20 degrees (left 70), extension 15 degrees (left 65). Finger flexion limited by pain and edema. **Weakness is NOT in a specific nerve distribution** — it is global and pain-limited, consistent with CRPS rather than nerve injury.\n- **Tremor**: Fine resting tremor of the right hand.\n- **Sudomotor**: Right palm notably more moist than left on palpation.\n- **Left upper extremity**: Completely normal examination.\n\n**Radiographs (right hand/wrist)**: Healed distal radius fracture in anatomic alignment. **Patchy periarticular osteopenia** of the carpals, metacarpals, and phalanges on the right — this is characteristic of CRPS (Sudeck atrophy).\n\n**Screening Exam**: No cervical radiculopathy signs. Negative Tinel at carpal tunnel and cubital tunnel. No thoracic outlet syndrome signs. Shoulder exam normal — rules out shoulder-hand syndrome variant.',
