    const Section(
      title: '+ Apply the Budapest criteria to this patient and discuss the utility of three-phase bone scan, thermography, and other diagnostic studies.',
      content: '**Budapest Criteria for CRPS (2010 IASP Revision)**:\n\nThe Budapest criteria replaced the earlier IASP criteria to improve specificity while maintaining sensitivity. Diagnosis requires ALL four of the following:\n\n**1. Continuing pain disproportionate to the inciting event** — MET\nHer fracture healed normally, yet she has severe burning pain rated 8-9/10 with allodynia — this is grossly disproportionate to a healed distal radius fracture.\n\n**2. Must report at least ONE symptom in THREE of the four categories**:\n- **Sensory**: Hyperesthesia (increased sensitivity) and/or allodynia — **MET** (she reports clothing, water, and light touch causing severe pain)\n- **Vasomotor**: Temperature asymmetry and/or skin color changes — **MET** (she reports color changes and the hand feels different temperature)\n- **Sudomotor/edema**: Edema and/or sweating changes — **MET** (she reports swelling)\n- **Motor/trophic**: Decreased ROM, weakness, tremor, dystonia, and/or trophic changes — **MET** (she reports inability to use the hand, weakness)\n\n**Result: Reports symptoms in 4/4 categories — criterion met.**\n\n**3. Must display at least ONE sign at time of evaluation in TWO or more categories**:\n- **Sensory**: Evidence of allodynia (cotton wisp test) and hyperalgesia (pinprick) — **MET**\n- **Vasomotor**: Temperature asymmetry >1C (measured at >2C) and visible color changes (erythema, mottling) — **MET**\n- **Sudomotor/edema**: Visible edema (2.3 cm difference) and increased sweating — **MET**\n- **Motor/trophic**: Decreased ROM, weakness, tremor, nail changes, hair changes, skin changes — **MET**\n\n**Result: Signs present in 4/4 categories — criterion met.**\n\n**4. No other diagnosis better explains the signs and symptoms** — **MET**\nCervical radiculopathy, peripheral nerve injury, carpal tunnel, thoracic outlet syndrome, and complex fracture have been excluded.\n\n**This patient meets ALL Budapest criteria for CRPS Type I (formerly Reflex Sympathetic Dystrophy).**\n\nCRPS Type I (no identified nerve injury) vs Type II (formerly causalgia — identifiable nerve injury). Her fracture did not involve a major named nerve, making this Type I.\n\n**Diagnostic Studies — Role and Limitations**:\n\n**Three-Phase Bone Scan (Technetium-99m)**:\n- **Classic finding**: Increased uptake in the affected extremity on all three phases — especially the delayed (third) phase showing diffuse periarticular uptake in the affected hand\n- **Sensitivity/Specificity**: Sensitivity 50-75%, specificity 80-90%. A positive bone scan supports the diagnosis but a NEGATIVE bone scan does NOT exclude CRPS. Sensitivity decreases with longer disease duration (the scan is most useful in the first 6 months).\n- **For this patient**: At 3 months, a bone scan may still be positive and can be useful for documentation purposes, especially for the workers\' compensation dispute.\n\n**Infrared Thermography**:\n- Measures temperature asymmetry objectively. Asymmetry >1C between affected and contralateral extremity is significant.\n- Can document vasomotor instability over time (serial measurements)\n- Useful for objective documentation but not diagnostic in isolation\n\n**Quantitative Sudomotor Axon Reflex Test (QSART)**:\n- Measures sudomotor (sweating) function. Can document autonomic dysfunction objectively.\n- Available at specialized autonomic testing laboratories.\n\n**Plain Radiographs**:\n- Already show patchy periarticular osteopenia — consistent with CRPS. This finding (Sudeck atrophy) takes weeks to develop and is relatively specific when present in the context of other CRPS features.\n\n**MRI**:\n- May show bone marrow edema and soft tissue enhancement in the affected region. Not routinely needed for diagnosis if Budapest criteria are met, but can help exclude other pathology.\n\n**Sympathetic Block (Stellate Ganglion Block)**:\n- Historically used as a diagnostic test for CRPS — pain relief after sympathetic block was considered confirmatory. This is now considered unreliable as a diagnostic test because (1) many CRPS patients do not have sympathetically maintained pain, and (2) the block has non-specific analgesic effects.\n- Better used therapeutically than diagnostically.\n\n**Key Point**: CRPS is a **clinical diagnosis** based on the Budapest criteria. Diagnostic studies are supportive and useful for documentation but are NOT required. No single test confirms or excludes CRPS.',
