    const Section(
      title: '+ Outline the multimodal treatment approach including graded motor imagery, desensitization, interventional options, and the role of spinal cord stimulation.',
      content: '**CRPS Treatment — Multimodal Interdisciplinary Approach**:\n\nTreatment must address pain, function, and psychological comorbidities simultaneously. A purely pharmacologic approach is insufficient.\n\n**Tier 1 — Rehabilitation-Based Interventions (Foundation of Treatment)**:\n\n**Graded Motor Imagery (GMI) — Three-Stage Program**:\nGMI is the most evidence-based rehabilitation intervention for CRPS. It works by gradually retraining the brain\'s cortical representation of the affected limb.\n1. **Stage 1 — Laterality Recognition**: Patient views images of left and right hands and identifies which side each image represents. This activates the premotor cortex without movement. Use the Recognise app or flash cards. Practice 3 times daily for 2 weeks.\n2. **Stage 2 — Imagined Movements**: Patient imagines moving the affected hand through various positions WITHOUT actually moving it. This activates motor planning networks. Practice 3 times daily for 2 weeks.\n3. **Stage 3 — Mirror Therapy**: Place a mirror between the hands. The patient moves the unaffected hand while watching its reflection, creating the visual illusion that the affected hand is moving painlessly. Gradually introduce movements of the affected hand behind the mirror. 10-15 minutes, 3-5 times daily.\n\n**Desensitization Program (OT-led)**:\n- Systematic graded exposure to tactile stimuli on the affected hand\n- Begin with textures the patient can tolerate (soft cotton, silk) and progressively advance to coarser textures (terrycloth, denim, Velcro)\n- Includes contrast baths (alternating warm and cool water immersion), fluidotherapy, vibration\n- Must be patient-directed — never force exposure beyond tolerance\n\n**Functional Restoration PT/OT**:\n- Stress-loading program: scrubbing (alternating compression) and carrying (sustained traction) exercises for the affected hand\n- Active ROM within pain tolerance — gentle, progressive\n- Edema management: retrograde massage, compression glove, elevation\n- Splinting: Resting splint for night use to maintain wrist in neutral. Dynamic splinting if joint contractures develop.\n\n**Tier 2 — Pharmacologic Management**:\n\n**Neuropathic Pain Medications**:\n- **Gabapentin**: Titrate to 1800-3600 mg/day in divided doses. First-line.\n- **Pregabalin**: Alternative to gabapentin. 75 mg BID, titrate to 150-300 mg BID.\n- **Duloxetine**: 60 mg daily — addresses both neuropathic pain and comorbid depression.\n- **Amitriptyline or nortriptyline**: 10-75 mg at bedtime. Analgesic and sleep benefit.\n\n**Anti-inflammatory/Bone Agents**:\n- **Bisphosphonates**: Alendronate or IV pamidronate — evidence supports use in CRPS for reducing pain and edema, especially in the first year. Mechanism may relate to inhibiting bone resorption and modulating inflammation.\n- **Oral corticosteroids**: Short course of prednisone (40 mg daily taper over 2-3 weeks) can be effective in early inflammatory CRPS with significant edema.\n\n**Topical Agents**:\n- **Topical ketamine/gabapentin/clonidine compound cream**: Applied to the affected area. Avoids systemic side effects.\n- **Lidocaine 5% patch**: Over the most painful area.\n- **Capsaicin 8% patch (Qutenza)**: Applied in-clinic under supervision. Can provide weeks of pain relief.\n\n**Avoid Opioids**: Opioids are generally ineffective for CRPS and carry risks of dependence, hyperalgesia, and functional impairment. If used at all, only short-term for severe flares.\n\n**Tier 3 — Interventional Procedures**:\n\n**Sympathetic Nerve Blocks**:\n- **Stellate ganglion block** (for upper extremity CRPS): Injection of local anesthetic near the stellate ganglion at C6-7 under fluoroscopic guidance. If the first block provides significant but temporary relief, a series of 3-6 blocks may produce cumulative benefit.\n- Response to sympathetic block helps classify the pain as sympathetically maintained (SMP) vs sympathetically independent (SIP) — this guides further treatment.\n\n**Spinal Cord Stimulation (SCS)**:\n- **Indication**: CRPS refractory to 3-6 months of conservative and pharmacologic treatment\n- **Mechanism**: Electrical stimulation of dorsal columns modulates pain signaling via the gate control theory and supraspinal mechanisms\n- **Evidence**: CRPS is one of the strongest indications for SCS. The KEMLER trial showed sustained improvement in pain and quality of life at 5 years. Newer high-frequency (10 kHz) and dorsal root ganglion (DRG) stimulation show particular promise for CRPS.\n- **DRG Stimulation**: Targets the dorsal root ganglion at the affected spinal level. More focal coverage than traditional SCS. FDA-approved for CRPS. May be superior to conventional SCS for extremity pain.\n- **Process**: Trial stimulation (percutaneous leads for 5-7 days) → if >50% pain relief → permanent implant.\n\n**Tier 4 — Advanced/Refractory Options**:\n- Intrathecal drug delivery (baclofen, ziconotide, opioid)\n- IV ketamine infusion (subanesthetic doses — emerging evidence, specialized centers)\n- Amputation is NOT recommended for CRPS — phantom limb pain and CRPS recurrence in the stump are common',
