    const Section(
      title: '+ How do you approach disability documentation for CRPS, and how do you advocate for a patient with an "invisible disability" in a workers\' compensation setting?',
      content: '**CRPS as an "Invisible Disability" — Challenges and Advocacy**:\n\nCRPS is one of the most commonly disbelieved chronic pain conditions because (1) the fracture has healed on X-ray, (2) objective findings can fluctuate, (3) the pain appears "disproportionate," and (4) many clinicians and adjusters are unfamiliar with the condition.\n\n**Disability Documentation Strategy**:\n\n**1. Establish the Diagnosis with Rigorous Documentation**:\n- Apply and document the Budapest criteria explicitly in the medical record — check each criterion and state whether it is met\n- Record objective examination findings at every visit: temperature asymmetry (measured with thermometer), edema (measured circumferentially), ROM (goniometer), grip strength (dynamometer), allodynia testing results\n- Photographs at each visit documenting color changes, edema, trophic changes\n- Supportive diagnostic studies: three-phase bone scan, thermography, radiographic osteopenia\n\n**2. Functional Capacity Documentation**:\n- Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE) by a certified evaluator — documents objective physical capabilities and limitations\n- Specific functional limitations: grip strength, pinch strength, sustained hand use, repetitive hand use, lifting capacity, fine motor tasks, typing speed and duration\n- Consistency testing within the FCE (measures of effort and reliability)\n\n**3. Medical Narrative Report**:\nWrite a comprehensive report for the workers\' compensation claim that includes:\n- Mechanism of injury and causal relationship to the workplace fall\n- Diagnosis with Budapest criteria application\n- Objective examination findings with measurements\n- Diagnostic study results\n- Treatment to date and response\n- Current functional limitations as they relate to her specific job demands (elementary school teacher)\n- Work capacity assessment: can she teach? With modifications? What restrictions?\n- Prognosis and expected future treatment needs\n- Statement of medical probability (more likely than not that CRPS resulted from the work injury)\n\n**4. Responding to the IME**:\n- The patient has the right to know who the IME examiner is and to have an observer present in some jurisdictions\n- Review the IME report when available — if it disputes the CRPS diagnosis, prepare a detailed rebuttal citing the Budapest criteria, objective findings, and supporting literature\n- The treating physician\'s opinion often carries significant weight because of the longitudinal treatment relationship\n\n**Advocacy for the Patient**:\n\n**Educating the Adjuster and Employer**:\n- Provide the workers\' compensation adjuster with educational materials about CRPS (IASP fact sheets, published clinical guidelines)\n- A letter from the physiatrist explaining that CRPS is a recognized, objective medical condition — not a psychological or malingered condition — carries clinical authority\n- Cite the Budapest criteria\'s specificity: the criteria were developed specifically to reduce over-diagnosis, so meeting them is meaningful\n\n**Addressing the "Normal X-Ray" Argument**:\n\'The fracture has healed, but CRPS is not a fracture. It is a disorder of the nervous system that was triggered by the fracture. The X-ray showing a healed bone is expected. CRPS is diagnosed by clinical criteria, not by X-ray, and this patient meets every diagnostic criterion.\'\n\n**Workplace Accommodations if Return to Work is Possible**:\n- Modified duty: reduced writing demands, use of left hand or voice-to-text, reduced classroom hours initially\n- Ergonomic assessment of the classroom and teaching materials\n- Gradual return-to-work plan with defined milestones\n- Job restructuring: can an aide handle tasks requiring dominant hand use?\n\n**If Unable to Return to Teaching**:\n- Vocational rehabilitation assessment for alternative employment\n- SSDI application if disability is expected to last >12 months\n- ADA protections for disability discrimination\n\n**Key Message**: The physiatrist serves as both the treating physician and the patient\'s medical advocate. Documenting CRPS rigorously and communicating the diagnosis in clear, objective terms is the most powerful advocacy tool.',
    ),
    const Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content: 'During a follow-up visit, the patient is distraught. She says: "My boss called me at home and said they don\'t believe I\'m really disabled. He said I should be ashamed of myself for faking an injury. My own sister told me it\'s all in my head. How can I prove to people that this is real?"',
