    const Section(
      title: '+ Demonstrate how you validate this patient\'s experience, address the stigma of an invisible disability, and empower her to advocate for herself.',
      content: '**Addressing Delegitimization of Pain — A Core Clinical Skill**:\n\nDelegitimization — when others question or deny the reality of a patient\'s suffering — is one of the most psychologically damaging experiences for people with chronic pain. It compounds the pain with shame, isolation, and self-doubt.\n\n**Step 1 — Unequivocal Validation**:\n\n\'I want to say something clearly, and I want you to hear it: your pain is real. CRPS is a medical condition recognized by every major medical organization in the world. It is not in your head, it is not exaggerated, and it is not something you are making up.\n\nI can see CRPS on your examination — the swelling, the color changes, the temperature difference, the way your hand reacts to light touch. These are objective, measurable findings. No one can fake a 2-degree temperature difference between their hands. No one can fake bone loss on an X-ray.\n\nYou have nothing to be ashamed of. Your boss is wrong. And your sister, as much as she may care about you, does not have the medical knowledge to make that judgment.\'\n\n**Step 2 — Educate to Empower**:\n\n\'Part of what makes CRPS so frustrating is that other people cannot see your pain. Your hand may look relatively normal to someone who does not know what to look for. But as your physician, I know exactly what I am seeing, and I want to give you tools to help the people in your life understand.\n\nI am going to do three things:\n1. Write a clear medical letter that you can share with your employer and your family explaining your diagnosis, what CRPS is, and what the objective medical findings are. This is not a letter asking for sympathy — it is a medical document stating facts.\n2. Give you educational materials from the International Association for the Study of Pain that explain CRPS in accessible language. You can share these with your sister, your friends, or anyone who needs to understand.\n3. Connect you with a CRPS support group — online or in person. Being around other people who share this experience can be incredibly validating.\'\n\n**Step 3 — Address the Workplace Issue Specifically**:\n\n\'What your boss did — calling you at home and accusing you of faking — may constitute workplace harassment of a person with a disability. You have legal protections under the Americans with Disabilities Act and under your state\'s workers\' compensation laws.\n\nI am going to recommend that you speak with a workers\' compensation attorney if you do not already have one. Your employer is not permitted to harass you for filing a legitimate claim. In the meantime, I will document this interaction in your medical record and include it in any future reports, because it is relevant to your psychological wellbeing and recovery.\'\n\n**Step 4 — Address the Self-Doubt**:\n\n\'I suspect that hearing these things from people you trust has made you start to wonder yourself — "Am I overreacting? Is it really that bad?" I want to name that feeling because it is incredibly common in people with invisible disabilities.\n\nYou are not overreacting. CRPS is rated as one of the most painful conditions in medicine — more painful than childbirth, more painful than amputation without anesthesia, in published pain severity rankings. The fact that you are functioning at all right now is a testament to your resilience, not evidence that the pain is not real.\'\n\n**Step 5 — Psychological Support**:\n\n\'I am also going to refer you to a psychologist who specializes in chronic pain — not because this is a psychological problem, but because dealing with constant pain AND being disbelieved by the people around you is an enormous psychological burden. You deserve professional support for both.\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Unequivocal medical validation, providing objective evidence the patient can share, empowering self-advocacy with education and documentation, addressing workplace harassment, naming and countering self-doubt, and providing psychological support without pathologizing.',
